From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 09:30:00 -0400
Subject: windows: the series builds and runs on Windows -- caption buttons,
 the row's colour, the rail

The first build of the series for a non-macOS target (docs/ARC-ROUND2.md,
round 8; M8 in docs/ROADMAP.md had not started). Nine portability errors in
four files, all one cause, and then the operator's look at the result --
five things, two more found while capturing them -- across the layout, the
address row's colour, the sidebar's rail and the expand-on-hover overlay:

- base::FilePath is std::wstring on Windows and std::string on the Mac, so
  every narrow-string Append("...") and FilePath("...") in the Arc importers,
  the sidebar backups and the screenshot file name failed to compile.
  FILE_PATH_LITERAL, FromUTF8Unsafe, AppendASCII and AsUTF8Unsafe, as
  Chromium's own code spells them; nothing changes on the Mac.
- Windows' caption buttons are frame-owned controls at the top RIGHT, over
  the address row's column, and the row ran under them. macOS's traffic
  lights sit over the sidebar's column, which is why the layout skipped the
  exclusion with a vertical strip. The row now stops short of the frame's
  trailing exclusion, the card's exposed top-right corner rounds, and with
  the row hidden (toolbar T8) the empty top container keeps the caption
  strip's height so the card cannot cover the buttons -- from the rail the
  window could not be closed at all. With no leading exclusion the sidebar's
  toggle takes the container's baseline instead of a centre computed from an
  empty box.
- Focused, the location bar borrows the results dropdown's background
  (LocationBarView::GetBackgroundColor while the caret shows), which the
  page-bar colour supplier never set: near-black on a white page's row. The
  dropdown's background, text, icons and selection follow the page too, as
  Safari's do, in the bar's contrast colour.
- A reload dropped the row to the window ground and left it there: the new
  Page starts with no colour, and WebContentsImpl announces a background
  colour only when it differs from the last one it SENT -- kept on the
  WebContents, not the Page -- so white reloading to white is never
  announced. The controller keeps its colour while the page loads and
  re-reads the Page at first paint and at the end of loading.
- Expanded on hover the strip floats over the page as a layer that does not
  fill its bounds opaquely, with a background made invisible so the window's
  gradient shows through (HANDOFF trap 8): every row read through to the
  page. The background is visible exactly while the strip is collapsed by
  state and drawn wider than the rail.
- The rail (sidebar Y6): the toggle, the Archived row and the New Tab row
  centre in the rail and take a size up, the Archived row as its icon alone;
  the Space switcher stacks its chips in a column with no spacers instead of
  a row that ran out of the rail and drew its "+" over the page; the
  downloads button and the switcher stack too. All keyed on the width being
  drawn, not the collapse state: expanded on hover the state stays collapsed
  while the rows are at the open width, and the first cut centred the toggle
  in a 350 DIP overlay.

The operator's reply the same afternoon, and one thing found on the way:

- The rail's tab pill was still 26 DIP wide, the "+" above it clipped to
  10 DIP by its own inset, the toggle 3.5 DIP right of the rows' centre.
  The width a rail row gets is decided in UnpinnedTabContainerViewLayout,
  not TabStripViewLayout, which is why overriding the strip's padding had
  changed nothing. Every container's side padding is stedding::
  kRailRowPadding, 3 DIP, in the rail -- keyed on the width being laid
  out -- and TabView::CollapsedWidth says the same 44, so a rail row is a
  44 DIP square around a 22 DIP favicon centred in the rail plus the
  gutter. The "+ New Tab" row takes the rail form from the region view
  (its plus alone, no inset), and the button containers sit on the same
  column (sidebar Y8; S-53).
- In the rail there was no address bar at all: the row hidden with the
  sidebar (toolbar T8) left an empty band under the caption buttons. The
  rule keeps the row while the frame's own buttons sit over its column
  (the trailing exclusion): the strip is there whatever the sidebar does.
  The toggle still wins; macOS is unchanged (toolbar T22, a unit test).
- A profile killed while the sidebar was collapsed never opened again:
  the crashed-session "Restore pages?" bubble anchors to the app menu
  button before the window is shown, the button is not drawn with the row
  hidden, and the fallback anchor is a tracked element the tracker does
  not know until the widget is visible -- CHECK, every launch. The button
  anchors such a bubble to the top container instead (toolbar T23).
- The bar stopped with the row's contents, short of the caption buttons,
  and its end read as a cut corner beside them (operator, second reply).
  The bar runs on under the buttons to the card's edge; the contents
  still stop short; the card's top-right corner rounds as on the Mac;
  the buttons' glyphs take the bar's contrast colour
  (kColorCaptionButtonOnToolbar from PageBarColorSupplier); and the
  address field centres on the bar, the layout handing the toolbar the
  reserve (toolbar T19, T18).
- Found while looking at that across window sizes: in a 900 DIP window
  with the sidebar open the field sat over the back, forward and reload
  buttons. Round 7's centring read the two spacers' bounds after the
  layout had dropped them for want of width -- hidden, at the origin --
  and clamped the field to x 0. It keeps the layout's position when the
  spacers are gone (toolbar T18).
- An "Action required" chip on the app menu: Chromium's ExternalRegistryLoader
  reads Google Chrome's own registry key whatever the branding, and
  Adobe's installer had put its Acrobat extension there, so the browser
  was fetching it from Google's update server and asking. The provider
  is not created on Windows (privacy Q9). The chip, for the errors that
  are real, is the toolbar buttons' height (toolbar T25).
- The caption buttons stretched from the window's top edge to the frame's
  height, their centre 4 DIP above the row's; they centre on the row's
  centre line at their own height (toolbar T19).

Measured on the Windows build against the mac probes (tooling/probes/
window.json at scale 1): 20 of 23 pass, the three others being state or
platform -- no essentials in a fresh profile, and the probe that expects the
row where Windows now has the caption strip. The card's edges and gutters
and its 12.00 DIP corner radius are the mac's to the pixel.

What this is not: a port. Branding is mac-gated, and the keyboard map, the
Cocoa menus and the keychain naming live in is_mac files.

One patch across features rather than fixups, as 0013 and 0037 were for
rounds 5 and 7: these hunks sit on lines several later patches rewrote.

Why: docs/ARC-ROUND2.md round 8, the operator's look at the first Windows
build (2026-09-08).
Removable when: M8 rebuilds the sidebar and the page card on upstream's
vertical tabs with the frame's exclusions handled there, and Chromium's own
importers and backups are used in place of ours.
